    {
     "name": "code-bug",
     "success": false,
     "actual_output": "# The Problem: Mutable Default Argument\n\n## What's Wrong\n\nThe issue is using a **mutable default argument** (`bucket=[]`). In Python, default argument values are evaluated **once** at function definition time, not each time the function is called. This means the same list object is shared across all calls that don't provide an explicit `bucket` argument.\n\n```python\ndef add_item(item, bucket=[]):\n    bucket.append(item)\n    return bucket\n\nprint(add_item(\"apple\"))   # ['apple']\nprint(add_item(\"banana\"))  # ['apple', 'banana']  \u2190 BUG! Expected ['banana']\nprint(add_item(\"cherry\"))  # ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']  \u2190 BUG!\n```\n\nEach call accumulates items in the **same** list because `bucket` always refers to the same list object created at definition time.\n\n## How to Fix It\n\n### \u2705 Recommended: Use `None` as the default\n\n```python\ndef add_item(item, bucket=None):\n    if bucket is None:\n        bucket = []\n    bucket.append(item)\n    return bucket\n```\n\nThis creates a **new** list for each call that doesn't provide a `bucket` argument, while still allowing callers to pass in an existing list if desired.\n\n### Why This Works\n\n| Approach | Behavior |\n|---|---|\n| `bucket=[]` | Same list reused across calls (bug) |\n| `bucket=None` + `if bucket is None: bucket = []` | New list created per call (correct) |\n\n### Key Takeaway\n\n> **Never use mutable objects** (lists, dicts, sets, etc.) as default argument values. Use `None` as the default and create the mutable object inside the function body. This is a well-known Python gotcha documented in the [official Python FAQ](https://docs.python.org/3/faq/programming.html#why-are-default-values-shared-between-objects).",
     "expected_output": "The default list is created once and shared between calls, so items accumulate across calls. Use bucket=None and create a new list inside the function when it is None.",
